# Normalize all text files to LF in the repository AND in the working tree.
#
# `eol=lf` (not just `text=auto`) is load-bearing on Windows: with git's
# core.autocrlf=true, `text=auto` alone stores LF but checks CRLF out, and
# Prettier - whose `endOfLine` default is "lf" - then reports every freshly
# checked-out file as unformatted. That makes `npm run format:check` fail
# locally while passing in CI, which is exactly the kind of it-works-on-CI
# split that hides real failures.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The one Windows-native script, exempted from the LF rule above. This file
# used to say "Nothing in this repo is a Windows-native script (no
# .bat/.cmd/.ps1), so LF everywhere is safe" - true until the Capacitor
# Android shell (#101) brought gradlew.bat in. cmd.exe's goto/label scanning
# is documented-unreliable in LF-only batch files, which is why Gradle's own
# repository pins *.bat to CRLF; no CI runner executes it, so only a Windows
# checkout would ever have noticed.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Images are binary - never munge their bytes.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

# So is a jar (the Gradle wrapper, #101 - the only one tracked). Content
# auto-detection happened to classify it right, but this file's own policy is
# to declare binary types rather than rely on that.
*.jar binary

# Large geospatial artifacts are binary too (these are gitignored today, but
# declare it so an accidental add can't corrupt one).
*.pmtiles binary
*.fgb binary
*.tif binary
*.tiff binary
